00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:05.750
Before that, we have our
cool things of the week.

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:08.890
The first one, it is
something quite cool.

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:13.620
It is related to Cloud IAM, so
Identity and Access Management.

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:16.280
And it is the fact that now
you can define your own custom

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:16.780
roles.

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:18.630
Until now, we have
had roles like--

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:21.790
a long time ago, we had
like project owner, editor,

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:22.840
and viewer.

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:24.285
And that was pretty much it.

00:01:24.285 --> 00:01:25.660
And then we started
having things

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:29.350
like now you can be
an App Engine editor,

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:31.360
or for specific things.

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:34.370
You can write into cloud
storage, or stuff like that,

00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:35.162
but not read.

00:01:35.162 --> 00:01:36.370
That doesn't make much sense.

00:01:36.370 --> 00:01:38.740
But you can read, but not
write, stuff like that.

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:40.510
Now, what you can do
is you can actually

00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:44.440
create new roles that
have specific permissions

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:47.000
and then apply
those to your users.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:48.889
So you can create
new ones, which

00:01:48.889 --> 00:01:50.930
is really powerful because,
in your organization,

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:53.680
you might have someone that
should be able to do something

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:58.240
really specific, and you want to
allow them to do that and just

00:01:58.240 --> 00:01:58.777
that.

00:01:58.777 --> 00:01:59.735
MARK MANDEL: It's nice.

00:01:59.735 --> 00:01:59.980
Yeah.

00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:00.896
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:02:00.896 --> 00:02:02.320
It's just things
that people need,

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:03.760
so it's good that it's there.

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:05.920
MARK MANDEL: I really like this
for ongoing maintenance, too.

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:06.520
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:07.510
MARK MANDEL: As new
products come out

00:02:07.510 --> 00:02:09.343
or maybe you realize
you missed a permission

00:02:09.343 --> 00:02:12.460
across a large swath of users,
if you assign them that role,

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:13.960
then you can just
change that role.

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:15.300
And that gives you a
lot of flexibility, too,

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:16.120
going forward.

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:17.520
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Did you
say a swath of users?

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:18.250
MARK MANDEL: A swath.

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:19.270
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's
such a cool word.

00:02:19.270 --> 00:02:20.030
Very good.

00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:21.617
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:21.617 --> 00:02:23.200
FRANCESC CAMPOY: The
second cool thing

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:24.574
of the week-- it's
something that

00:02:24.574 --> 00:02:26.210
is really, really
cool-- it is mixing

00:02:26.210 --> 00:02:29.500
the two hot things of the year.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:30.250
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:31.833
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
It's mixing Cloud ML,

00:02:31.833 --> 00:02:35.110
so machine learning,
with serverless.

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:38.200
So the title of the these
"Medium" blog posts,

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:41.200
it's "Serverless Transferred
Learning with Cloud ML Engine

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:42.460
and Keras."

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:45.392
And it is really interesting
from at least the point

00:02:45.392 --> 00:02:46.600
of view of transfer learning.

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:48.355
Transfer learning, if you
don't know what it is--

00:02:48.355 --> 00:02:48.820
MARK MANDEL: No idea.

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:50.195
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Basically, it is

00:02:50.195 --> 00:02:52.150
you're able to
learn things better

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:53.626
once you know other things.

00:02:53.626 --> 00:02:54.500
That's a whole point.

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:57.580
Like, if you know
how to play tennis,

00:02:57.580 --> 00:03:00.924
learning how to play table
tennis, it's easier, right?

00:03:00.924 --> 00:03:01.590
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:03.280
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So that's
basically the whole idea

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:04.720
applied to neural networks.

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:07.734
So the idea here is that
they use Inception, I think.

00:03:07.734 --> 00:03:09.150
Yeah, they use
Inception, which is

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:10.750
a neural network
that has been trained

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:12.746
to recognize a lot of things.

00:03:12.746 --> 00:03:14.620
Like, that's basically
what we use internally

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:15.910
for Google Photos.

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:18.560
Not the same, but we use
something quite similar.

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:23.230
What you can do is draw the
last part of the neural network

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:25.180
and then retrain it
to learn something

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:28.810
slightly different-- like to
train, is this an elephant?

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:30.700
Or things like that,
specific things

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:34.060
that are not necessarily what
the neural network was designed

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:34.720
to.

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:36.520
So you can adapt it
to your use case.

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:39.610
So he talks about how
to do this in Keras.

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:42.241
Keras is just another
machine learning library.

00:03:42.241 --> 00:03:42.990
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:43.770
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Not TensorFlow.

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:44.130
MARK MANDEL: Got it.

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:45.296
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It's Keras.

00:03:45.296 --> 00:03:46.246
It's a different one.

00:03:46.246 --> 00:03:48.370
And the cool thing is that,
then, what they do is--

00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:52.570
OK, so now that we've
designed this neural network,

00:03:52.570 --> 00:03:53.620
how do we train it?

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:55.661
And rather than training
it locally, what they do

00:03:55.661 --> 00:03:57.019
is that use Cloud ML Engine.

00:03:57.019 --> 00:03:59.560
And Cloud ML Engine, that's the
serverless part of the thing,

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:00.100
right?

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:01.980
You just send it there,
and it's trained.

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:04.396
You don't need to care about
how many machines are running

00:04:04.396 --> 00:04:06.280
or anything, it just happens.

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:07.960
And not only that,
but then, afterwards,

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:11.020
once you have the model,
you can just click a button

00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:12.250
and start serving it.

00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:15.560
So then you also have
serverless predictions.

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:18.230
So it is all serverless
and all machine learning,

00:04:18.230 --> 00:04:19.950
and it's an awesome blog post.

00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:21.425
So go check it out.

00:04:21.425 --> 00:04:23.800
MARK MANDEL: And it's also
written by a community member.

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:27.035
So I'm going to massacre
this person's name.

00:04:27.035 --> 00:04:28.160
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Let's see.

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:29.860
MARK MANDEL: Hayato Yoshikawa.

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:32.110
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I'd say
that is a pretty good effort.

00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:32.860
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:34.040
So it's pretty good.

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:35.590
And speaking of
community articles,

00:04:35.590 --> 00:04:38.059
I've got a second
one here from--

00:04:38.059 --> 00:04:39.100
this is probably easier--

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:42.400
Mark Betz, SRE at Olark.

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:44.980
He has an article written
called "Five Things

00:04:44.980 --> 00:04:47.182
Learned using Terraform
to Manage Google Cloud

00:04:47.182 --> 00:04:49.390
Infrastructure," or even
just "Cloud Infrastructure,"

00:04:49.390 --> 00:04:50.230
actually.

00:04:50.230 --> 00:04:52.040
So if you're not
familiar with Terraform,

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:54.940
it's an automation solution
for managing infrastructure

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:56.080
in the cloud.

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:56.980
I love automation.

00:04:56.980 --> 00:04:59.438
I keep going on about deployment
manager, things like that.

00:04:59.438 --> 00:05:02.170
He's basically written down five
different things that he thinks

00:05:02.170 --> 00:05:04.740
are good best practices
for using Terraform

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:06.459
with Google Cloud
and some practices

00:05:06.459 --> 00:05:08.000
that you can take
advantage of there.

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:10.290
It's worth having read to see
if it's going to apply to you.

00:05:10.290 --> 00:05:12.060
I always advocate that
people use automation

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:14.160
when setting up their cloud
infrastructure because things

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:15.660
go wrong, and it's always
good when you can tear them

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:16.890
up and tear them down easily.

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:18.104
So yes, go have a read.

00:05:18.104 --> 00:05:19.020
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:20.030
It's a great blog post.

00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:22.159
But my favorite part,
do you know what it is?

