00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:54.120
advocate at Google
joining us today,

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:55.814
though not from
cloud specifically,

00:05:55.814 --> 00:05:57.230
but we'll let him
talk about that.

00:05:57.230 --> 00:05:59.260
David East, thank you
so much for joining us.

00:05:59.260 --> 00:06:00.400
How are you doing today?

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:01.440
DAVID EAST: Doing great.

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:02.280
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:03.730
Thank you so much
for joining us today.

00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:05.480
We're going to have a
chat about how Cloud

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:06.810
Functions and Firebase Hosting.

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:08.310
But before we do
that, why don't you

00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:11.400
tell us a little bit about
what you do at the Googles?

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:12.900
DAVID EAST: So as
you probably know,

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:15.050
the Google is a search company.

00:06:15.050 --> 00:06:18.270
Go to the website and type
in what you want to learn.

00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:21.930
But it also does other things,
like Cloud-based stuff outside

00:06:21.930 --> 00:06:22.980
of Google Cloud.

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:26.790
So I work on Firebase, which
is a mobile app platform

00:06:26.790 --> 00:06:29.130
for Android, iOS, and web.

00:06:29.130 --> 00:06:31.450
And I primarily work on
the web side of things,

00:06:31.450 --> 00:06:34.560
so I work on things like the
real-time database, which

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:36.240
is a way for you to
store and sync data

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:39.390
in real time without having
to run your own server;

00:06:39.390 --> 00:06:41.970
Cloud Functions, which is
a way of writing server

00:06:41.970 --> 00:06:43.950
code without having
to have a server;

00:06:43.950 --> 00:06:47.100
and also Firebase
Hosting, which is really

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:50.040
a first-class web
hosting experience that's

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:51.664
kind of built for performance.

00:06:51.664 --> 00:06:52.580
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:06:52.580 --> 00:06:53.880
So we've had many--

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:57.120
well, many-- a bunch of
episodes on Firebase, I'd say.

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:00.030
We even had once this
guy called David East

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:04.410
that answered questions of the
week back then in episode 15.

00:07:04.410 --> 00:07:06.610
DAVID EAST: I
wouldn't trust him.

00:07:06.610 --> 00:07:08.340
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And
the thing that we

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:10.140
want to talk about
today, specifically,

00:07:10.140 --> 00:07:11.710
it's two different things.

00:07:11.710 --> 00:07:13.500
One is Firebase
Hosting, which you

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:15.720
mentioned real quick right
now, and Cloud Functions

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:16.830
for Firebase.

00:07:16.830 --> 00:07:20.520
And I don't really know
anything about any of those.

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:23.201
I know about Firebase, like the
database and authentication,

00:07:23.201 --> 00:07:25.450
which is really cool, but
not really Firebase Hosting.

00:07:25.450 --> 00:07:27.270
So why don't you
tell us a little bit

00:07:27.270 --> 00:07:28.680
about what is it really?

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:33.150
DAVID EAST: Firebase Hosting,
it's really near and dear to me

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:37.410
because I joined
Firebase back in 2014,

00:07:37.410 --> 00:07:38.880
before it was at Google.

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:41.250
And my first
project was actually

00:07:41.250 --> 00:07:43.290
to help ship Firebase Hosting.

00:07:43.290 --> 00:07:46.140
So it's kind of like a
special thing for me.

00:07:46.140 --> 00:07:48.894
And when we were coming up with
the idea for Firebase Hosting,

00:07:48.894 --> 00:07:50.310
we had a lot of
people at the time

00:07:50.310 --> 00:07:52.989
who were using real-time
database and authentication

00:07:52.989 --> 00:07:54.780
to build their apps,
but they didn't really

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:57.330
have any place to host them--
so if you're a web app.

00:07:57.330 --> 00:07:59.580
And we had a lot of people
reach out and be like, hey,

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:01.567
can you make this
any easier for us?

00:08:01.567 --> 00:08:03.650
So rather than just kind
of creating a place where

00:08:03.650 --> 00:08:06.510
you can throw up your files,
we kind of thought, all right,

00:08:06.510 --> 00:08:11.310
what could we do to make hosting
not just easy for deployment,

00:08:11.310 --> 00:08:13.660
but also like really fast?

00:08:13.660 --> 00:08:15.630
So what we did was we
came up with this service

00:08:15.630 --> 00:08:19.350
where you pretty much install
a command line using NPM.

00:08:19.350 --> 00:08:21.810
You do a little initialization.

00:08:21.810 --> 00:08:25.140
But then, with just one
command doing Firebase deploy,

00:08:25.140 --> 00:08:27.990
we will automatically
just upload those files,

00:08:27.990 --> 00:08:31.440
and we will deploy them
out onto our server.

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:34.919
But we actually don't actually
serve from the server,

00:08:34.919 --> 00:08:36.900
because we're backed by a CDN.

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:40.210
So you get low latency
all around the globe.

00:08:40.210 --> 00:08:42.750
And then you don't have to
worry about things like SSL,

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:45.125
because we automatically hook
up SSL certificate for you.

00:08:45.125 --> 00:08:47.208
You don't have to worry
about going out and buying

00:08:47.208 --> 00:08:48.480
one or provisioning yourself.

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:50.500
That just sort of happens.

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:53.550
And one of the things we also
do is provide a really easy way

00:08:53.550 --> 00:08:55.650
to integrate your own
custom domain into it.

00:08:55.650 --> 00:08:58.320
So just kind of like, without
really having to do much,

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:02.550
you can get a full site
deployed, really performant,

00:09:02.550 --> 00:09:03.660
really easy.

00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:06.480
And you kind of get all
these things out of the box.

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:07.396
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:09:07.396 --> 00:09:10.630
So whenever I want to
deploy a website myself,

00:09:10.630 --> 00:09:12.390
I never use Firebase Hosting.

00:09:12.390 --> 00:09:15.300
So normally, what
I do is either I

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:19.560
use App Engine, which allows
me also to have custom domains,

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:21.810
or I use Google Cloud Storage.

00:09:21.810 --> 00:09:23.610
Where is Firebase Hosting?

00:09:23.610 --> 00:09:25.540
Is it dynamic, like App Engine?

00:09:25.540 --> 00:09:27.030
Is it more like static storage?

00:09:27.030 --> 00:09:28.530
DAVID EAST: Well,
that's really what

00:09:28.530 --> 00:09:29.740
I'm here to talk about today.

00:09:29.740 --> 00:09:31.710
So when we first started
with Firebase Hosting,

00:09:31.710 --> 00:09:34.530
we were really only looking
at doing static, so pretty

00:09:34.530 --> 00:09:36.210
much all static files.

00:09:36.210 --> 00:09:39.060
There's a lot you can do
with a static website.

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:41.550
Everyone kind of reaches
for server code at first,

00:09:41.550 --> 00:09:43.350
but you'd be really,
really amazed

00:09:43.350 --> 00:09:46.140
at the things you can do with
static generators and stuff

00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:47.260
like that.

00:09:47.260 --> 00:09:50.100
So for years, we
were only static.

00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:52.605
And that's why we were able
to back everything by a CDN

00:09:52.605 --> 00:09:54.480
because we didn't have
to worry about running

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:56.340
any code on the server.

00:09:56.340 --> 00:10:00.270
But, I guess last March, when
we launched Cloud Functions,

00:10:00.270 --> 00:10:02.226
this really opened
up the door for us

00:10:02.226 --> 00:10:04.600
to provide an integration with
Firebase Hosting and Cloud

00:10:04.600 --> 00:10:05.110
Functions.

00:10:05.110 --> 00:10:07.360
And we had been kind
of experimenting

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:10.660
with this for a bit during
our own internal hack weeks.

00:10:10.660 --> 00:10:13.330
And the team lead
for Firebase Hosting,

00:10:13.330 --> 00:10:17.560
Michael Bleigh, he came up with
this crazy system at the time

00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:21.370
where you would basically
create a Cloud Function,

00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:23.350
generate some content
with it, and then

00:10:23.350 --> 00:10:26.030
you would send that back
to Firebase Hosting, which

00:10:26.030 --> 00:10:29.365
would then cache that
content on the CDN

00:10:29.365 --> 00:10:31.220
and then back to the user.

00:10:31.220 --> 00:10:33.670
And so when the user would
then request the site again,

00:10:33.670 --> 00:10:36.160
or another user in
that CDN Edge area,

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:38.350
it wouldn't actually go
back to Cloud Functions

00:10:38.350 --> 00:10:39.620
to regenerate it again.

00:10:39.620 --> 00:10:41.860
It would just serve
from the CDN Edge.

00:10:41.860 --> 00:10:45.370
And we would specify how long
it would live in the Edge.

00:10:45.370 --> 00:10:49.330
But it became sort of like the
first time we saw, oh, wow, we

00:10:49.330 --> 00:10:54.074
can provide this dynamic content
with this static twist to it.

00:10:54.074 --> 00:10:55.990
MARK MANDEL: So you
mentioned something there,

00:10:55.990 --> 00:10:56.722
Cloud Functions.

00:10:56.722 --> 00:10:58.180
We did do an episode
a little while

00:10:58.180 --> 00:11:00.790
ago, Episode 73
with Bret McGowan,

00:11:00.790 --> 00:11:03.130
but this is Cloud
Functions for Firebase.

00:11:03.130 --> 00:11:04.379
Is that the same thing?

00:11:04.379 --> 00:11:05.920
For people who don't
know what it is,

00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:07.330
can you explain what it
is and the differences?

00:11:07.330 --> 00:11:08.560
It would be really great.

00:11:08.560 --> 00:11:09.268
DAVID EAST: Yeah.

00:11:09.268 --> 00:11:11.620
So Cloud Functions is
basically serverless

00:11:11.620 --> 00:11:13.480
compute, server
code, and don't have

00:11:13.480 --> 00:11:14.800
to manage your own servers.

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:19.000
And Cloud Functions for
Firebase is this Firebase layer

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:19.700
on top of that.

00:11:19.700 --> 00:11:22.240
So it's still Cloud
Functions, the cloud product.

00:11:22.240 --> 00:11:25.630
And Cloud Functions for Firebase
is an SDK that we provide you.

00:11:25.630 --> 00:11:29.740
So essentially, if you want to
respond to events that happen

00:11:29.740 --> 00:11:32.500
in your Firebase ecosystem--
so if something changes

00:11:32.500 --> 00:11:35.950
in your database, a user logs
in, you get an event from

00:11:35.950 --> 00:11:39.010
Firebase Analytics, or someone
uploads a file from cloud

00:11:39.010 --> 00:11:39.970
storage--

00:11:39.970 --> 00:11:44.020
you can respond to these events
and then run server code.

00:11:44.020 --> 00:11:46.660
And so what we've done
with Cloud Functions

00:11:46.660 --> 00:11:49.240
and Firebase Hosting is
we've provided a way to say,

00:11:49.240 --> 00:11:53.140
whenever someone hits
this URL for your website,

00:11:53.140 --> 00:11:54.730
let's run this code.

00:11:54.730 --> 00:11:58.090
And then, when you are
done generating this code,

00:11:58.090 --> 00:12:01.000
use a cache control header
and specify how long you want

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:02.980
it to store in the CDN Edge.

00:12:02.980 --> 00:12:03.730
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:12:03.730 --> 00:12:04.230
All right.

00:12:04.230 --> 00:12:08.290
So when we put together Cloud
Functions with static hosting,

00:12:08.290 --> 00:12:10.606
what sort of things
can you build?

00:12:10.606 --> 00:12:11.730
Like, what's possible here?

00:12:11.730 --> 00:12:14.470
DAVID EAST: Well, with Cloud
Functions and Firebase Hosting,

00:12:14.470 --> 00:12:18.670
there's pretty much not much
you can't do because now you

00:12:18.670 --> 00:12:22.300
have the ability to do
dynamic server content

00:12:22.300 --> 00:12:25.840
but still present it to
the user as static content.

00:12:25.840 --> 00:12:29.290
So before, pretty much whenever
you'd do Firebase deploy,

00:12:29.290 --> 00:12:30.550
that was kind of it for you.

00:12:30.550 --> 00:12:31.420
You would deploy it out.

00:12:31.420 --> 00:12:33.503
All your static files are
going to go out the web.

00:12:33.503 --> 00:12:35.770
And if you realize you
had a typo on a page,

00:12:35.770 --> 00:12:37.480
or wrote a bug, or
something like that,

00:12:37.480 --> 00:12:40.600
you'd have to go out and write
Firebase deploy again and just

00:12:40.600 --> 00:12:42.670
redeploy your entire website.

00:12:42.670 --> 00:12:44.230
Now, what you can
do is you can still

00:12:44.230 --> 00:12:46.180
have a bunch of static pages.

00:12:46.180 --> 00:12:48.580
You can break it up to say,
OK, this section of my site

00:12:48.580 --> 00:12:51.460
is static, but this
page specifically

00:12:51.460 --> 00:12:53.740
or these set of
pages are dynamic.

00:12:53.740 --> 00:12:59.230
And at that point, effectively,
whatever a dynamic site can do

00:12:59.230 --> 00:13:02.402
is exactly what Cloud Functions
and Firebase Hosting can do.

00:13:02.402 --> 00:13:03.610
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Interesting.

00:13:03.610 --> 00:13:06.400
So what I'm thinking
about now is

00:13:06.400 --> 00:13:08.750
this would be a really
good idea for someone that

00:13:08.750 --> 00:13:11.440
starts with a static
site, and then they

00:13:11.440 --> 00:13:14.140
want to add more dynamic
things, like, let's say,

00:13:14.140 --> 00:13:15.790
sending email or
something like that.

00:13:15.790 --> 00:13:16.770
Is that the use case?

00:13:16.770 --> 00:13:18.340
Or do you see also
people building

00:13:18.340 --> 00:13:22.812
from scratch on Firebase
plus Cloud Functions?

00:13:22.812 --> 00:13:24.520
DAVID EAST: I think
you're totally right.

00:13:24.520 --> 00:13:26.590
I think if you start
out with a static site,

00:13:26.590 --> 00:13:30.700
and you might be building like
a portfolio page or something

00:13:30.700 --> 00:13:33.310
like e-commerce where
you deploy out all

00:13:33.310 --> 00:13:34.780
of your static-generated pages--

00:13:34.780 --> 00:13:37.540
because if it's static, it's
going to be super, super fast--

00:13:37.540 --> 00:13:40.330
but maybe you have a certain
page that is dynamic.

00:13:40.330 --> 00:13:43.600
So the register, or the shopping
cart, or something like that

00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:47.890
is dynamic, but the profile
pages or the recipe pages,

00:13:47.890 --> 00:13:49.730
or something like
that, is all static.

00:13:49.730 --> 00:13:51.820
So it's a nice
mix-and-match approach.

00:13:51.820 --> 00:13:55.630
But another place that I
see where you kind of just

00:13:55.630 --> 00:14:00.250
go dynamic to start with is, if
you're building any JavaScript

00:14:00.250 --> 00:14:02.680
framework apps or single-page
applications and stuff like,

00:14:02.680 --> 00:14:05.740
that you might want to take
advantage of server-side

00:14:05.740 --> 00:14:06.970
rendering those.

00:14:06.970 --> 00:14:09.850
And that is pretty easy
because, at that point,

00:14:09.850 --> 00:14:13.140
you've created your whole
app that typically would

00:14:13.140 --> 00:14:15.620
be client rendered at first.

00:14:15.620 --> 00:14:18.730
But with just whatever
frameworks, library code,

00:14:18.730 --> 00:14:20.800
you can deploy that
out to the server.

00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:24.430
It generates the static HTML and
sends that down to the browser.

00:14:27.280 --> 00:14:34.000
I've done the Angular,
Universal, React, Preact, xJS,

00:14:34.000 --> 00:14:37.300
Vue.js, all these
server-side rendering apps.

00:14:37.300 --> 00:14:40.810
It takes pretty much
like 10 lines of code

00:14:40.810 --> 00:14:42.294
or so to get it integrated.

00:14:42.294 --> 00:14:43.210
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:14:43.210 --> 00:14:46.060
So if I understand correctly
then, what you're saying

00:14:46.060 --> 00:14:48.790
is that we have all
the static hosting

00:14:48.790 --> 00:14:52.030
and that it's
accessible from a CDN.

00:14:52.030 --> 00:14:54.910
So it means low latency
and good performance.

00:14:54.910 --> 00:14:56.410
And then we have
the Cloud Functions

00:14:56.410 --> 00:14:59.560
that are going to generate
the dynamic content

00:14:59.560 --> 00:15:02.440
or react to whatever
the user is doing.

00:15:02.440 --> 00:15:05.630
And those things
are run somewhere

00:15:05.630 --> 00:15:09.374
on Google Cloud Platform, and
then the results are cached?

00:15:09.374 --> 00:15:10.040
DAVID EAST: Yes.

00:15:10.040 --> 00:15:13.030
So essentially, the cache
is controlled by you

00:15:13.030 --> 00:15:14.810
through a cache control header.

00:15:14.810 --> 00:15:18.220
Cloud Functions right
now is all in Node.js,

00:15:18.220 --> 00:15:22.835
so we use Express to do
the server responses.

00:15:22.835 --> 00:15:25.780
So if you're building
an Express.js app,

00:15:25.780 --> 00:15:29.200
it's probably about a
handful of lines of code

00:15:29.200 --> 00:15:32.080
to just move it over
to Cloud Functions.

00:15:32.080 --> 00:15:34.450
But essentially, when you're
ready to send your response

00:15:34.450 --> 00:15:35.200
back--

00:15:35.200 --> 00:15:37.170
so you get a request
from the user,

00:15:37.170 --> 00:15:39.910
you generate your content,
however you do that--

00:15:39.910 --> 00:15:41.500
and then right
before you send back,

00:15:41.500 --> 00:15:43.390
you just set a cache
control header.

00:15:43.390 --> 00:15:46.930
And you essentially will say,
OK, it's a public header.

00:15:46.930 --> 00:15:50.740
It has a maxage of this is how
long the browser will cache it,

00:15:50.740 --> 00:15:53.620
but then there is
s-maxage which says

00:15:53.620 --> 00:15:57.040
this will sit for this
many seconds on the server

00:15:57.040 --> 00:15:58.660
or on the CDN.

00:15:58.660 --> 00:16:01.375
So in that area, if
one person requests,

00:16:01.375 --> 00:16:04.940
it's going to generate it and
do that whole longer round trip.

00:16:04.940 --> 00:16:07.570
But then the second time,
the second user, they're

00:16:07.570 --> 00:16:09.650
just going to go
straight out to the Edge.

00:16:09.650 --> 00:16:12.160
And the Edge is going to have
it, and send it right back,

00:16:12.160 --> 00:16:14.510
and avoid running the
Cloud Function again.

00:16:14.510 --> 00:16:16.218
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
then, I imagine, that

00:16:16.218 --> 00:16:19.480
is very simple from the code
side of things to control.

00:16:19.480 --> 00:16:22.360
For instance, this is something
I never want to cache.

00:16:22.360 --> 00:16:24.697
Just simply do not
set those headers,

00:16:24.697 --> 00:16:25.780
and that's pretty much it?

00:16:25.780 --> 00:16:26.170
DAVID EAST: Yeah.

00:16:26.170 --> 00:16:26.950
That's really it.

00:16:26.950 --> 00:16:28.960
And there's even
more advanced things

00:16:28.960 --> 00:16:31.660
you can do with the headers
that are really easy to do.

00:16:31.660 --> 00:16:34.960
There's the
stale-while-revalidate header,

00:16:34.960 --> 00:16:37.610
which is really cool
for certain use cases.

00:16:37.610 --> 00:16:42.550
So if a user makes a request
out to your site and the CDN

00:16:42.550 --> 00:16:45.625
notices that, well,
this content is stale,

00:16:45.625 --> 00:16:47.500
but what I'm going to
do is I'm going to send

00:16:47.500 --> 00:16:49.210
them back the stale version.

00:16:49.210 --> 00:16:50.330
And in the background.

00:16:50.330 --> 00:16:52.990
I'm going to go
out to your origin

00:16:52.990 --> 00:16:55.630
or to your Cloud Function,
generate that content,

00:16:55.630 --> 00:16:57.800
and store it back
into the cache.

00:16:57.800 --> 00:16:59.350
So the user got
a quick response.

00:16:59.350 --> 00:17:01.540
It might be stale
content, but the next user

00:17:01.540 --> 00:17:04.720
or the next request will
bring back the fresh content.

00:17:04.720 --> 00:17:06.700
And so if you know you
can kind of get away

00:17:06.700 --> 00:17:08.980
with serving up
stale content, it's

00:17:08.980 --> 00:17:12.280
a really great technique
to give good performance

00:17:12.280 --> 00:17:14.545
and also keep that cache warm.

00:17:14.545 --> 00:17:16.420
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So that
is very interesting,

00:17:16.420 --> 00:17:20.589
and it brings me to cache
validation, which is a very

00:17:20.589 --> 00:17:22.089
cool problem, we all know.

00:17:22.089 --> 00:17:24.280
How do you invalidate the cache?

00:17:24.280 --> 00:17:26.170
Let's say that you
say, well, this content

00:17:26.170 --> 00:17:28.119
is valid for an hour,
and then something

00:17:28.119 --> 00:17:29.920
happens that invalidates it.

00:17:29.920 --> 00:17:32.170
Is there any way to
tell whatever servers

00:17:32.170 --> 00:17:33.820
are caching that
information that they

00:17:33.820 --> 00:17:35.504
should ask for the new version?

00:17:35.504 --> 00:17:37.420
DAVID EAST: Firebase
Hosting, from the get go,

00:17:37.420 --> 00:17:41.860
has really strong cache
and validation strategies--

00:17:41.860 --> 00:17:45.100
or I guess really just one
cache validation strategy.

00:17:45.100 --> 00:17:47.680
Essentially, with
static hosting,

00:17:47.680 --> 00:17:50.140
whenever you say Firebase
deploy, everything

00:17:50.140 --> 00:17:52.990
you deploy is in
one atomic version.

00:17:52.990 --> 00:17:55.030
If you go to the
Firebase console,

00:17:55.030 --> 00:17:56.920
you can actually see
in the Hosting tab

00:17:56.920 --> 00:18:00.940
every single version of your
app that you've ever deployed.

00:18:00.940 --> 00:18:03.880
And you actually can roll
back to any single one

00:18:03.880 --> 00:18:05.410
of those just with one click.

00:18:05.410 --> 00:18:07.760
And it's almost instant.

00:18:07.760 --> 00:18:11.500
And why we're able to do that
is because, since we have all

00:18:11.500 --> 00:18:13.840
these atomic versions,
whenever you deploy out

00:18:13.840 --> 00:18:16.215
we're able to contact
each edge and say, hey,

00:18:16.215 --> 00:18:17.590
there's a new
version right here.

00:18:17.590 --> 00:18:22.075
Just purge everything in
your cache because the user

00:18:22.075 --> 00:18:23.450
is going to want
the new version.

00:18:23.450 --> 00:18:25.900
And there's nothing
stopping you from doing that

00:18:25.900 --> 00:18:28.060
with Cloud Functions
and Firebase Hosting.

00:18:28.060 --> 00:18:30.564
So let's say you have like
really aggressive caching

00:18:30.564 --> 00:18:32.230
going on in your Cloud
Function, and you

00:18:32.230 --> 00:18:34.815
want to cache for half a
day or something like that

00:18:34.815 --> 00:18:36.940
because you maybe publish
like two blog posts a day

00:18:36.940 --> 00:18:37.960
or something.

00:18:37.960 --> 00:18:40.990
And you realize, oh, I want
to publish this sooner,

00:18:40.990 --> 00:18:42.790
or there's a typo or
something like that.

00:18:42.790 --> 00:18:45.910
When you write Firebase deploy,
it's going to purge the cache.

00:18:45.910 --> 00:18:48.040
And even those cache
control headers

00:18:48.040 --> 00:18:49.639
are going to be
ignored at that point.

00:18:49.639 --> 00:18:51.430
And it's going to know
to go back and fetch

00:18:51.430 --> 00:18:52.510
the fresh content.

00:18:52.510 --> 00:18:54.010
MARK MANDEL: So
when you deploy, you

00:18:54.010 --> 00:18:55.551
said, as a single
unit, that contains

00:18:55.551 --> 00:18:58.420
both the static files and your
Cloud Functions for Firebase?

00:18:58.420 --> 00:18:59.110
Is it both?

00:18:59.110 --> 00:19:00.235
DAVID EAST: Great question.

00:19:00.235 --> 00:19:02.830
So you have to kind of
break it up into two pieces

00:19:02.830 --> 00:19:06.010
because the Cloud
Functions are deployed out

00:19:06.010 --> 00:19:10.930
to their own place, and the
same with Firebase Hosting.

00:19:10.930 --> 00:19:13.420
So they're actually in
two separate places,

00:19:13.420 --> 00:19:16.060
which is a really nice
way to kind of break up

00:19:16.060 --> 00:19:18.100
the two parts of your app.

00:19:18.100 --> 00:19:21.010
And you can deploy
them at the same time,

00:19:21.010 --> 00:19:23.257
or you can specify
one for the other

00:19:23.257 --> 00:19:24.340
and whatever really helps.

00:19:24.340 --> 00:19:26.506
Because if you just need
to update your server code,

00:19:26.506 --> 00:19:28.740
that's not going to
break your cache.

00:19:28.740 --> 00:19:31.300
So if you're just trying
to keep your cache strategy

00:19:31.300 --> 00:19:33.680
kind of just humming along,
just deploy your server code.

00:19:33.680 --> 00:19:36.221
But if you need to kind of break
the world because everything

00:19:36.221 --> 00:19:38.460
needs to be updated, you
have that option, too.

00:19:38.460 --> 00:19:38.910
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:19:38.910 --> 00:19:39.409
All right.

00:19:39.409 --> 00:19:41.682
So this sounds hugely powerful.

00:19:41.682 --> 00:19:43.390
For a long time, I've
been using probably

00:19:43.390 --> 00:19:46.220
App Engine for more of my quote
unquote "serverless" needs.

00:19:46.220 --> 00:19:48.190
Does this mean I shouldn't
be using App Engine?

00:19:48.190 --> 00:19:49.870
Is there like a
sweet spot for this?

00:19:49.870 --> 00:19:52.250
Where should I choose maybe
one thing over another?

00:19:52.250 --> 00:19:55.620
DAVID EAST: App Engine has a
lot of really cool add-ons.

00:19:55.620 --> 00:19:58.660
And when you're doing things
with longer-lived tasks,

00:19:58.660 --> 00:20:00.044
I would definitely
start thinking

00:20:00.044 --> 00:20:01.210
about doing your own server.

00:20:01.210 --> 00:20:03.820
So any really heavy
compute options,

00:20:03.820 --> 00:20:06.380
or just if you're running a
Cron or something like that

00:20:06.380 --> 00:20:08.180
on your server,
then App Engine's

00:20:08.180 --> 00:20:09.900
definitely the
great place there.

00:20:09.900 --> 00:20:11.900
But when you're just
doing very simple things,

00:20:11.900 --> 00:20:15.440
like if all you want to do is
build a Handlebars app where

00:20:15.440 --> 00:20:18.320
you just have some type of
server templating where you

00:20:18.320 --> 00:20:20.420
say, I have some data,
I have a template,

00:20:20.420 --> 00:20:23.570
let's generate my HTML, this is
a really great place for that

00:20:23.570 --> 00:20:26.510
because it's also just trivial,
like we were talking about

00:20:26.510 --> 00:20:29.120
before, to integrate
the caching into it.

00:20:29.120 --> 00:20:32.180
So it's this really nice place
for traditional web development

00:20:32.180 --> 00:20:32.840
with it.

00:20:32.840 --> 00:20:37.149
And also modern,
front-end framework

00:20:37.149 --> 00:20:38.690
developing with
server-side rendering

00:20:38.690 --> 00:20:40.460
is also a really
good spot for this

00:20:40.460 --> 00:20:42.920
because one of the most
expensive parts when you're

00:20:42.920 --> 00:20:45.320
doing server side rendering
with JavaScript frameworks

00:20:45.320 --> 00:20:47.870
is that regeneration
every single time.

00:20:47.870 --> 00:20:52.310
And for most people's websites,
how often do they update?

00:20:52.310 --> 00:20:56.060
Unless you're a big,
real-time, live site,

00:20:56.060 --> 00:20:59.930
your content might only change
a handful of times a day.

00:20:59.930 --> 00:21:02.660
So it doesn't make sense
to just always regenerate.

00:21:02.660 --> 00:21:05.769
And this is really where that
caching strategy can help you

00:21:05.769 --> 00:21:07.310
because it keeps
that content cached,

00:21:07.310 --> 00:21:09.726
and so you don't have to keep
paying that server cost over

00:21:09.726 --> 00:21:10.880
and over again.

00:21:10.880 --> 00:21:11.713
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:21:11.713 --> 00:21:14.180
So now I'm thinking,
let's imagine

00:21:14.180 --> 00:21:18.380
that I'm trying to build
a to-do app because that's

00:21:18.380 --> 00:21:19.910
what everybody builds.

00:21:19.910 --> 00:21:22.040
I want to store my
information somewhere.

00:21:22.040 --> 00:21:23.360
How would this work?

00:21:23.360 --> 00:21:27.020
You would have Firebase
Hosting calling

00:21:27.020 --> 00:21:29.330
Cloud Functions for
Firebase and then

00:21:29.330 --> 00:21:31.880
that accessing some database?

00:21:31.880 --> 00:21:35.270
Or would you recommend directly
using Firebase database?

00:21:35.270 --> 00:21:36.740
Where do you store
the information?

00:21:36.740 --> 00:21:37.730
How do you decide that?

00:21:37.730 --> 00:21:42.440
DAVID EAST: So essentially, you
can store it wherever you want.

00:21:42.440 --> 00:21:44.090
You can just stick
it in a JSON file

00:21:44.090 --> 00:21:46.790
and upload it with
your Cloud Functions.

00:21:46.790 --> 00:21:48.180
Not the best option.

00:21:48.180 --> 00:21:51.840
But one of the things we do
that makes it really easy

00:21:51.840 --> 00:21:55.010
is we ship the Firebase
admin SDK, which

00:21:55.010 --> 00:21:58.910
is this really nice
server SDK for controlling

00:21:58.910 --> 00:22:01.400
all these different things
with your Firebase accounts.

00:22:01.400 --> 00:22:04.430
And so you get a greater
set of permissions

00:22:04.430 --> 00:22:07.700
that you'd expect to do as
the owner of your project.

00:22:07.700 --> 00:22:10.850
And what we have is ways
to say, OK, I'm the owner.

00:22:10.850 --> 00:22:13.160
I want to go read a
database from this section,

00:22:13.160 --> 00:22:16.850
and I want to check out my
authenticated users here.

00:22:16.850 --> 00:22:18.950
And so when someone
makes a request,

00:22:18.950 --> 00:22:21.785
you're likely going to use
some type of stateful URL.

00:22:21.785 --> 00:22:23.780
So whatever route
parameter, you can just

00:22:23.780 --> 00:22:27.560
use that to request that data
using the Firebase admin SDK,

00:22:27.560 --> 00:22:30.920
get that data back, and then
just template your page,

00:22:30.920 --> 00:22:32.390
do whatever's needed.

00:22:32.390 --> 00:22:34.720
And so you can use
whatever you want,

00:22:34.720 --> 00:22:36.590
but we've made it
really easy to talk

00:22:36.590 --> 00:22:39.200
to Firebase database,
or cloud storage,

00:22:39.200 --> 00:22:40.380
or something like that.

00:22:40.380 --> 00:22:41.210
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So then, that means

00:22:41.210 --> 00:22:43.460
that you can store the
information wherever you want.

00:22:43.460 --> 00:22:46.260
And if you store information
on Firebase database,

00:22:46.260 --> 00:22:48.860
then the Cloud
Functions for Firebase

00:22:48.860 --> 00:22:52.400
do have access to that
Firebase database.

00:22:52.400 --> 00:22:53.450
Is that the case?

00:22:53.450 --> 00:22:54.158
DAVID EAST: Yeah.

00:22:54.158 --> 00:22:56.150
When Cloud Functions
boots up, your credentials

00:22:56.150 --> 00:22:58.730
are automatically configured
in that environment.

00:22:58.730 --> 00:23:00.890
So it's actually your
Google Cloud credentials.

00:23:00.890 --> 00:23:06.090
So whatever that project has
access to, you can tap into.

00:23:06.090 --> 00:23:08.180
So if you're using
the Cloud Vision API,

00:23:08.180 --> 00:23:09.650
those credentials
are automatically

00:23:09.650 --> 00:23:11.240
configured in that environment.

00:23:11.240 --> 00:23:13.730
And the same for your
Firebase credentials.

00:23:13.730 --> 00:23:16.320
And so it's really
easy to just say, OK,

00:23:16.320 --> 00:23:19.010
I'm going to read data from
this section and send it back,

00:23:19.010 --> 00:23:21.500
or I need to call
this API, I need

00:23:21.500 --> 00:23:23.900
to talk to whatever service
that's in the Google Cloud

00:23:23.900 --> 00:23:25.820
or Firebase ecosystem.

00:23:25.820 --> 00:23:27.950
All that authentication
and credentialing

00:23:27.950 --> 00:23:30.070
is just automatically
taken care of for you.

00:23:30.070 --> 00:23:30.620
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:23:30.620 --> 00:23:32.953
MARK MANDEL: So that's almost
burying the lead a little.

00:23:32.953 --> 00:23:34.730
I feel like I want
to highlight it.

00:23:34.730 --> 00:23:37.030
This seems like a sweet
spot where maybe you

00:23:37.030 --> 00:23:38.510
want to cross ecosystems.

00:23:38.510 --> 00:23:41.399
Like say you want to do
some stuff in Google Cloud

00:23:41.399 --> 00:23:43.190
Platform-- maybe you
have a database there,

00:23:43.190 --> 00:23:44.810
or you want to do something
like Cloud Vision--

00:23:44.810 --> 00:23:46.400
but also be able
to take advantage

00:23:46.400 --> 00:23:48.860
of basically the buttery
sweetness of Firebase.

00:23:48.860 --> 00:23:50.220
Would that be kind of accurate?

00:23:50.220 --> 00:23:50.928
DAVID EAST: Yeah.

00:23:50.928 --> 00:23:51.470
Totally.

00:23:51.470 --> 00:23:53.510
The fact that you don't have
to worry about your credentials

00:23:53.510 --> 00:23:54.230
is really great.

00:23:54.230 --> 00:23:56.270
And we also provide
this credentialing

00:23:56.270 --> 00:23:59.820
with the Firebase CLI that
you install through NPM.

00:23:59.820 --> 00:24:01.310
We have local runners.

00:24:01.310 --> 00:24:04.694
So a lot of times, you're
dealing with, oh, I just

00:24:04.694 --> 00:24:06.110
want to do this
locally right now.

00:24:06.110 --> 00:24:07.680
I don't want to have
to deploy to test this.

00:24:07.680 --> 00:24:09.430
Well, we have ways of
testing that locally

00:24:09.430 --> 00:24:10.870
with the same credentialing.

00:24:10.870 --> 00:24:13.010
But yeah, if you're trying
to talk to Cloud Vision,

00:24:13.010 --> 00:24:14.420
you're going to need a server.

00:24:14.420 --> 00:24:17.220
And typically, those tend
to not take too long.

00:24:17.220 --> 00:24:20.030
And so Cloud Functions and
doing stuff like Cloud Vision

00:24:20.030 --> 00:24:22.280
is an amazing sweet spot.

00:24:22.280 --> 00:24:23.090
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:24:23.090 --> 00:24:24.560
So now I'm curious.

00:24:24.560 --> 00:24:26.390
Have you seen any
particular awesome things

00:24:26.390 --> 00:24:27.348
that people have built?

00:24:27.348 --> 00:24:29.480
Or are there particularly
amazing examples

00:24:29.480 --> 00:24:30.900
that you'd like
to highlight here?

00:24:30.900 --> 00:24:33.740
DAVID EAST: So I've been doing a
series on server-side rendering

00:24:33.740 --> 00:24:34.975
using JavaScript frameworks.

00:24:34.975 --> 00:24:37.100
And it was sort of interesting
because I have a lot

00:24:37.100 --> 00:24:39.320
of friends who make frameworks.

00:24:39.320 --> 00:24:42.800
I've done a 20% project
on the Angular team,

00:24:42.800 --> 00:24:45.500
and I talk to a
lot of other people

00:24:45.500 --> 00:24:47.930
who are like Ember
team and other teams.

00:24:47.930 --> 00:24:50.540
And also, I have a lot of
friends on the Chrome team.

00:24:50.540 --> 00:24:54.590
And they have this interesting
approach to doing things

00:24:54.590 --> 00:24:55.580
for web performance.

00:24:55.580 --> 00:24:57.410
And some of my friends
on the Chrome team

00:24:57.410 --> 00:25:01.350
aren't totally convinced that
server-side rendering is going

00:25:01.350 --> 00:25:04.140
to be better for you, rather
than just sort of booting up

00:25:04.140 --> 00:25:09.600
from scratch because it's a
lot of tax on the main process.

00:25:09.600 --> 00:25:12.720
And so, one of the things I went
out to do was is kind of figure

00:25:12.720 --> 00:25:15.000
out, OK, what's the truth here?

00:25:15.000 --> 00:25:16.890
What is really the
performance story?

00:25:16.890 --> 00:25:18.420
And so what I kind
of found out is

00:25:18.420 --> 00:25:21.090
that, if you're doing
server-side rendering

00:25:21.090 --> 00:25:25.950
wrong, yeah, it's going
to make you think slower.

00:25:25.950 --> 00:25:28.590
You're going to see the
page a lot faster, maybe,

00:25:28.590 --> 00:25:32.050
because your time to first bite
actually might be well worse.

00:25:32.050 --> 00:25:35.160
But what I realized is, when
you bring all these things

00:25:35.160 --> 00:25:38.027
in together, you can actually
get an amazing performance

00:25:38.027 --> 00:25:40.110
story out of server-side
rendering your JavaScript

00:25:40.110 --> 00:25:41.730
framework on Cloud Functions.

00:25:41.730 --> 00:25:43.290
Because, if you're
just serving up

00:25:43.290 --> 00:25:47.640
this dynamic content from the
CDN, and all of your assets

00:25:47.640 --> 00:25:49.620
are being served from
the CDN, and you're not

00:25:49.620 --> 00:25:54.570
making any client-side network
requests to template your data,

00:25:54.570 --> 00:25:57.000
there tends to be, even on
a really low-power device,

00:25:57.000 --> 00:25:59.280
I have seen really
fast load times.

00:25:59.280 --> 00:26:01.570
And that's something that
I haven't seen in the past.

00:26:01.570 --> 00:26:03.570
A lot of people server-side
rendered their apps.

00:26:03.570 --> 00:26:07.230
And they used to serve it over
a CDN or something static,

00:26:07.230 --> 00:26:09.180
but now they have to
use a dynamic server.

00:26:09.180 --> 00:26:12.279
And it can take a second to
just generate their content.

00:26:12.279 --> 00:26:14.070
And that's already a
second before the user

00:26:14.070 --> 00:26:15.730
sees anything on the page.

00:26:15.730 --> 00:26:18.150
So as far as
amazing things, I've

00:26:18.150 --> 00:26:20.970
just seen the performance
implications out of this

00:26:20.970 --> 00:26:22.860
is really, really cool.

00:26:22.860 --> 00:26:26.950
And the amount of code that it
takes is just kind of amazing.

00:26:26.950 --> 00:26:28.950
I've seen like 20
lines, 30 lines of code

00:26:28.950 --> 00:26:30.340
to implement the whole thing.

00:26:30.340 --> 00:26:31.470
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:26:31.470 --> 00:26:33.502
So that sounds awesome.

00:26:33.502 --> 00:26:35.085
You said that you
were doing a series.

00:26:35.085 --> 00:26:37.020
Is that a series on YouTube?

00:26:37.020 --> 00:26:37.920
What's the name?

00:26:37.920 --> 00:26:38.628
DAVID EAST: Yeah.

00:26:38.628 --> 00:26:41.340
So on the Firebase
YouTube channel,

00:26:41.340 --> 00:26:45.060
just YouTube.com/Firebase,
there's a whole series that

00:26:45.060 --> 00:26:47.780
I'm currently running called,
"Server-side Rendering with

00:26:47.780 --> 00:26:49.050
JavaScript Frameworks."

00:26:49.050 --> 00:26:51.360
And the whole thing
is that we take a look

00:26:51.360 --> 00:26:54.420
one framework at a time
how you would set this up.

00:26:54.420 --> 00:26:57.540
And then we follow up with
a companion video that

00:26:57.540 --> 00:26:59.530
is a performance profiling.

00:26:59.530 --> 00:27:01.860
So we take a look
at what tools you

00:27:01.860 --> 00:27:04.459
use to profile the
performance of your website

00:27:04.459 --> 00:27:06.000
or your server-side
rendered website.

00:27:06.000 --> 00:27:08.359
And we take a look
at how fast is

00:27:08.359 --> 00:27:09.900
the non-server-side
rendered version?

00:27:09.900 --> 00:27:11.940
How fast is the server-side
rendered version?

00:27:11.940 --> 00:27:13.920
And we don't just take
a look at numbers.

00:27:13.920 --> 00:27:16.050
I go into Tools
like web page tests,

00:27:16.050 --> 00:27:19.330
which allows you to run real
devices around the world.

00:27:19.330 --> 00:27:22.200
And you can simulate
really slow networks.

00:27:22.200 --> 00:27:26.370
And then they give you basically
the Chrome DevTools view up

00:27:26.370 --> 00:27:27.120
on the website.

00:27:27.120 --> 00:27:29.640
And they also give you lots
of other great kind of graphs

00:27:29.640 --> 00:27:33.840
and profiles where you can see,
how hard was the CPU working

00:27:33.840 --> 00:27:34.620
on the device?

00:27:34.620 --> 00:27:36.330
And when was your first paint?

00:27:36.330 --> 00:27:38.040
And when were you fully loaded?

00:27:38.040 --> 00:27:40.110
And so we kind of
step through each one

00:27:40.110 --> 00:27:42.000
of those metrics
on each version,

00:27:42.000 --> 00:27:43.765
and we see which
one came out on top.

00:27:43.765 --> 00:27:44.640
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:27:44.640 --> 00:27:46.266
So that YouTube
series sounds amazing.

00:27:46.266 --> 00:27:47.640
Are there any
other places people

00:27:47.640 --> 00:27:50.410
should go if this stuff sounds
particularly interesting?

00:27:50.410 --> 00:27:54.070
DAVID EAST: I actually did a
Kickstarter course on this.

00:27:54.070 --> 00:27:55.620
Or I called it a crash course.

00:27:55.620 --> 00:27:57.990
So also on the Firebase
YouTube channel,

00:27:57.990 --> 00:28:03.000
it's called like "Node.js Crash
Course on Firebase Hosting,"

00:28:03.000 --> 00:28:04.390
or something like that.

00:28:04.390 --> 00:28:07.650
And that is essentially
like, hey, I've

00:28:07.650 --> 00:28:08.760
never done this before.

00:28:08.760 --> 00:28:09.900
How do I get started?

00:28:09.900 --> 00:28:14.710
And I go from blank folder
to fully-deployed website

00:28:14.710 --> 00:28:16.810
with Cloud Functions
and Firebase Hosting.

00:28:16.810 --> 00:28:18.240
So that's a really good spot.

00:28:18.240 --> 00:28:20.220
And also, the
official documentation

00:28:20.220 --> 00:28:21.120
is really good, too.

00:28:21.120 --> 00:28:24.450
So if you're sort of
a Firebase Hosting pro

00:28:24.450 --> 00:28:26.580
and you've used Cloud
Functions before,

00:28:26.580 --> 00:28:29.040
it's really going to
be just like one or two

00:28:29.040 --> 00:28:32.100
lines of configuration to get
them talking to each other.

00:28:32.100 --> 00:28:34.530
So definitely, the
official documentation

00:28:34.530 --> 00:28:35.899
is a really good place as well.

00:28:35.899 --> 00:28:36.940
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Awesome.

00:28:36.940 --> 00:28:39.460
Well, we've kind
of run out of time,

00:28:39.460 --> 00:28:41.490
but I'd like to give
you the opportunity

00:28:41.490 --> 00:28:44.544
to plug anything else that
you'd like to tell our audience.

00:28:44.544 --> 00:28:45.210
DAVID EAST: Yes.

00:28:45.210 --> 00:28:47.841
We just shipped Cloud
Firestore, which

00:28:47.841 --> 00:28:50.340
is something that we've been
working on really, really hard.

00:28:50.340 --> 00:28:54.870
And honestly, just from my
own personal standpoint,

00:28:54.870 --> 00:28:56.460
I'm really, really
excited about it

00:28:56.460 --> 00:29:00.360
because now you can do a lot
more complex querying, which

00:29:00.360 --> 00:29:03.600
is something that the real-time
database was limited on.

00:29:03.600 --> 00:29:08.880
And for me, I'm just
mind-blown that we can finally

00:29:08.880 --> 00:29:12.489
do completely-persisted
offline for web.

00:29:12.489 --> 00:29:14.280
So if you're building
progressive web apps,

00:29:14.280 --> 00:29:16.030
it's kind of amazing
that you kind of just

00:29:16.030 --> 00:29:19.050
enable persistence in
your Cloud Firestore app,

00:29:19.050 --> 00:29:20.337
and then that's it.

00:29:20.337 --> 00:29:22.170
So if you have a service
worker and the user

00:29:22.170 --> 00:29:24.990
goes offline, boom,
you are still up

00:29:24.990 --> 00:29:27.570
and running with all of your
data from Cloud Firestore.

00:29:27.570 --> 00:29:29.920
And then when they
go back online,

00:29:29.920 --> 00:29:32.940
all the data gets resynced,
and conflict resolution

00:29:32.940 --> 00:29:35.020
is automatically
handled for you.

00:29:35.020 --> 00:29:36.600
So it's kind of amazing.

00:29:36.600 --> 00:29:39.840
I feel like we're in this total
brand new world of development

00:29:39.840 --> 00:29:42.720
where I can use Cloud
Firestore to store all my data,

00:29:42.720 --> 00:29:45.800
use Cloud Functions and
Firebase Hosting to do

00:29:45.800 --> 00:29:48.277
all my dynamic content
or my static content.

00:29:48.277 --> 00:29:50.610
And it's kind of like the web
that we were promised back

00:29:50.610 --> 00:29:52.220
in HTML5 days.

00:29:52.220 --> 00:29:53.762
I can kind of start
seeing it happen.

00:29:53.762 --> 00:29:54.803
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Awesome.

00:29:54.803 --> 00:29:56.580
Well, that sounds
really, really cool.

00:29:56.580 --> 00:29:58.350
And who knows, maybe
we have something

00:29:58.350 --> 00:30:01.540
to mention also about it with
some episode or something.

00:30:01.540 --> 00:30:04.270
But that's a secret.

00:30:04.270 --> 00:30:07.180
Anyway, thanks so much, David,
for taking the time today.

00:30:07.180 --> 00:30:07.920
DAVID EAST: Thank you,
so much, for having me.

00:30:07.920 --> 00:30:09.040
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It
was amazing to discover

00:30:09.040 --> 00:30:10.170
what Cloud Functions is.

00:30:10.170 --> 00:30:10.740
Thank you.

00:30:10.740 --> 00:30:11.050
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:30:11.050 --> 00:30:11.310
Thanks so much, David.

00:30:11.310 --> 00:30:11.346
DAVID EAST: All right.

00:30:11.346 --> 00:30:12.380
Have a good one, guys.

00:30:12.380 --> 00:30:14.380
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So thanks
again, to David East,

00:30:14.380 --> 00:30:16.929
for coming back to the podcast
